{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass, when all the men of war were consumed, etc., and HaShem spoke to me, saying.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): once it became clearly known to Israel that all the men of war had finished dying, from twenty years old and upward, then the divine speech was singled out to me, and the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded us to cross the border of Moab. And know that the view of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, is that the divine speech was not singled out to Moses until the men of the wilderness had all perished, for his greatness was on account of Israel. Israel had been rebuked all thirty-eight years, and the day on which this became known to them was the fifteenth of Av, when the moon was full, as is stated in tractate Ta'anit, and that day was great among Israel with great joy, for on it they knew that the decree had been annulled; also for the man Moses it was exceedingly great, with the return of prophecy to him, for on it the divine speech was singled out to him. And the reason it was not singled out to him immediately when the decree was completed on the tenth, is that Moses was mourning over the decree for seven days, from the ninth to the fifteenth, and prophecy does not rest amid sorrow but amid joy; and for this reason it was not singled out to him until the fifteenth. And from this time onward all would be joyful together — Moses and the children of Israel. And therefore we do not find the expression וידבר from when Moses sent the spies until here, but only the expression ויאמר, because he did not prophesy through the clear speculum (aspaklaria ha-me'irah). And here the masters of truth revealed to us the superiority of the expression וידבר over the expression ויאמר, and hence they said: ״וידבר ה' אלי״ — ״to me״ was the dibbur (speech), meaning: now, in the דבור and not previously, but only in אמירה. And from this you will understand the matter of Jeremiah, who said (Jeremiah 1:6–7): ״ואומר אהה ה' אלהים הנה לא ידעתי דבר כי נער אנכי״ — he means that he had not attained the level of דבור, which is the clear speculum, but only the speculum that does not shine; and therefore he mentioned the name א-ד followed by י-ה, which is the speculum that does not shine, the attribute of judgment in which there is mercy, and this is the attainment of all the prophets; and therefore it immediately appended to him (ibid. verse 7): ״ויאמר ה' אלי״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:44:59.319337876Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}